<?xml version='1.0' encoding='UTF-8' standalone='yes'?>
<p:sld xmlns:a="http://schemas.openxmlformats.org/drawingml/2006/main" xmlns:p="http://schemas.openxmlformats.org/presentationml/2006/main" xmlns:r="http://schemas.openxmlformats.org/officeDocument/2006/relationships"><p:cSld><p:spTree><p:nvGrpSpPr><p:cNvPr id="1" name=""/><p:cNvGrpSpPr/><p:nvPr/></p:nvGrpSpPr><p:grpSpPr/><p:sp><p:nvSpPr><p:cNvPr id="2" name="TextBox 1"/><p:cNvSpPr txBox="1"/><p:nvPr/></p:nvSpPr><p:spPr><a:xfrm><a:off x="648000" y="396000"/><a:ext cx="10800000" cy="324000"/></a:xfrm><a:prstGeom prst="rect"><a:avLst/></a:prstGeom><a:noFill/></p:spPr><p:txBody><a:bodyPr wrap="none"><a:spAutoFit/></a:bodyPr><a:lstStyle/><a:p><a:r><a:rPr sz="1100" b="1"><a:solidFill><a:srgbClr val="666666"/></a:solidFill></a:rPr><a:t>A DECISION, AND ITS PAYOFF</a:t></a:r></a:p></p:txBody></p:sp><p:sp><p:nvSpPr><p:cNvPr id="3" name="TextBox 2"/><p:cNvSpPr txBox="1"/><p:nvPr/></p:nvSpPr><p:spPr><a:xfrm><a:off x="648000" y="684000"/><a:ext cx="10800000" cy="648000"/></a:xfrm><a:prstGeom prst="rect"><a:avLst/></a:prstGeom><a:noFill/></p:spPr><p:txBody><a:bodyPr wrap="none"><a:spAutoFit/></a:bodyPr><a:lstStyle/><a:p><a:r><a:rPr sz="3000" b="1"><a:solidFill><a:srgbClr val="160933"/></a:solidFill></a:rPr><a:t>Why PostgreSQL, and why it mattered</a:t></a:r></a:p></p:txBody></p:sp><p:sp><p:nvSpPr><p:cNvPr id="4" name="TextBox 3"/><p:cNvSpPr txBox="1"/><p:nvPr/></p:nvSpPr><p:spPr><a:xfrm><a:off x="648000" y="1584000"/><a:ext cx="10800000" cy="4320000"/></a:xfrm><a:prstGeom prst="rect"><a:avLst/></a:prstGeom><a:noFill/></p:spPr><p:txBody><a:bodyPr wrap="square"><a:spAutoFit/></a:bodyPr><a:lstStyle/><a:p><a:pPr><a:spcAft><a:spcPts val="1000"/></a:spcAft></a:pPr><a:r><a:rPr sz="1500" b="1"><a:solidFill><a:srgbClr val="222222"/></a:solidFill></a:rPr><a:t>We compared PostgreSQL against MongoDB. The argument for a document store is schema flexibility. The argument against, here, is that our hardest requirements are constraint-shaped.</a:t></a:r></a:p><a:p><a:pPr><a:spcAft><a:spcPts val="1000"/></a:spcAft></a:pPr><a:r><a:rPr sz="1500" b="0"><a:solidFill><a:srgbClr val="222222"/></a:solidFill></a:rPr><a:t></a:t></a:r></a:p><a:p><a:pPr><a:spcAft><a:spcPts val="1000"/></a:spcAft></a:pPr><a:r><a:rPr sz="1500" b="0"><a:solidFill><a:srgbClr val="222222"/></a:solidFill></a:rPr><a:t>A run must not sit in a state its decisions do not justify. A report version must not be submitted before it was created. One line of SQL each — application code everywhere else.</a:t></a:r></a:p><a:p><a:pPr><a:spcAft><a:spcPts val="1000"/></a:spcAft></a:pPr><a:r><a:rPr sz="1500" b="0"><a:solidFill><a:srgbClr val="222222"/></a:solidFill></a:rPr><a:t></a:t></a:r></a:p><a:p><a:pPr><a:spcAft><a:spcPts val="1000"/></a:spcAft></a:pPr><a:r><a:rPr sz="1500" b="1"><a:solidFill><a:srgbClr val="222222"/></a:solidFill></a:rPr><a:t>The payoff, months later: an end-to-end test started failing intermittently. The CHECK constraint submitted_at &gt;= created_at was refusing rows. The constraint was right — the host clock and the database container clock were 0.44 seconds apart, so records really were dated after they were stored.</a:t></a:r></a:p><a:p><a:pPr><a:spcAft><a:spcPts val="1000"/></a:spcAft></a:pPr><a:r><a:rPr sz="1500" b="0"><a:solidFill><a:srgbClr val="222222"/></a:solidFill></a:rPr><a:t></a:t></a:r></a:p><a:p><a:pPr><a:spcAft><a:spcPts val="1000"/></a:spcAft></a:pPr><a:r><a:rPr sz="1500" b="1"><a:solidFill><a:srgbClr val="222222"/></a:solidFill></a:rPr><a:t>We chose the database that could refuse. It refused something real.</a:t></a:r></a:p></p:txBody></p:sp></p:spTree></p:cSld><p:clrMapOvr><a:masterClrMapping/></p:clrMapOvr></p:sld>